<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="#Publication_11">
  <rdf:type rdf:resource="#Publication"/>
  <reportsReaction rdf:resource="#reaction_instance_11"/>
  <rdfs:label rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Publication_11</rdfs:label>
  <openalexId rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">W2247675138</openalexId>
  <score rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">0.9879362</score>
  <publicationDate rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2011-01-01</publicationDate>
  <rdfs:comment rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Title: Fischer-tropsch catalyst activation for enhanced product selectivity</rdfs:comment>
  <rdfs:comment rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Australia has large reserves of natural gas (195,000PJ (GA, 2009)), including conventional and unconventional resources. This ranks Australia as the 6th largest holder of natural gas reserves in Asia and the 12th largest in the world (CIA factbook and EIA). Converting part of these resources into easily transportable liquids (synthetic fuels) through the Gas to Liquid process (GTL) would decrease Australia dependency on imported oil and could help monetise gas resources not currently considered commercial, such as stranded and off-shore (associated) gas resources, but also tight gas, shale gas, and coal seam gas resources. In a typical GTL process, natural gas is converted into syngas (CO and H2), which is then transformed into aliphatic hydrocarbons, by way of Fischer-Tropsch Synthesis (FTS). FTS is a relatively unselective process in that it yields a broad distribution of hydrocarbon products from methane to heavy waxes (C1 to C100+). While gaseous hydrocarbons (short chains) generally need recycling and further processing to produce longer hydrocarbons, hydrocracking of the waxy, heavy ends is required to maximise the desired liquid products.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

