<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="#Publication_24">
  <rdf:type rdf:resource="#Publication"/>
  <reportsReaction rdf:resource="#reaction_instance_24"/>
  <rdfs:label rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Publication_24</rdfs:label>
  <openalexId rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">W4367149205</openalexId>
  <score rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">0.98303705</score>
  <publicationDate rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2021-12-06</publicationDate>
  <doi rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">10.1021/cen-09944-scicon4</doi>
  <rdfs:comment rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">DOI: 10.1021/cen-09944-scicon4</rdfs:comment>
  <rdfs:comment rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Title: A simply smashing ammonia synthesis</rdfs:comment>
  <rdfs:comment rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">A reactor that uses steel balls to smash together nitrogen, hydrogen, and an iron catalyst can produce a continuous stream of ammonia, even at room temperature and atmospheric pressure. The discovery comes hard on the heels of other recently published mechanochemical methods for ammonia synthesis from research groups seeking environmentally friendly alternatives to the leviathan Haber-Bosch process. Haber-Bosch combines N 2 and H 2 over an iron catalyst at high pressures and temperatures; it makes about 150 million tons of ammonia per year globally, largely for fertilizer production. It also consumes vast amounts of energy and causes roughly 1% of human-made CO 2 emissions. Most of those emissions come from the production of H 2 from methane or other fossil fuels. Researchers have spent decades investigating alternative ammonia syntheses based on electrochemistry, photochemistry, plasma-based processes, and molecular catalysts that mimic those involved in biological nitrogen fixation. These approaches still face</rdfs:comment>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

