<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="#Publication_32">
  <rdf:type rdf:resource="#Publication"/>
  <reportsReaction rdf:resource="#reaction_instance_32"/>
  <rdfs:label rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Publication_32</rdfs:label>
  <openalexId rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">W2475590109</openalexId>
  <score rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">0.9852316</score>
  <publicationDate rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2014-01-01</publicationDate>
  <doi rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">10.1055/sos-sd-212-00055</doi>
  <rdfs:comment rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">DOI: 10.1055/sos-sd-212-00055</rdfs:comment>
  <rdfs:comment rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Title: 1.1.3 Tandem Hydroformylation of Alkenes</rdfs:comment>
  <rdfs:comment rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Abstract This overview encompasses the most recent developments in the field of tandem hydroformylation reactions up to late 2012. The combination of the hydroformylation of alkenes and a subsequent transformation of the corresponding aldehydes gives access to a broad range of functionalities, relevant for both academy and industry. The chapter is subdivided according to the type of functionalization following the hydroformylation step.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

