<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="#Publication_44">
  <rdf:type rdf:resource="#Publication"/>
  <reportsReaction rdf:resource="#reaction_instance_44"/>
  <rdfs:label rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Publication_44</rdfs:label>
  <openalexId rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">W2059203845</openalexId>
  <score rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">0.9832436</score>
  <publicationDate rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">1954-10-01</publicationDate>
  <doi rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">10.1002/jctb.5010041005</doi>
  <rdfs:comment rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">DOI: 10.1002/jctb.5010041005</rdfs:comment>
  <rdfs:comment rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Title: The production of oxalic acid from new zealand plant wastes</rdfs:comment>
  <rdfs:comment rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Abstract Recent work has shown that oxalic acid is capable of replacing sulphuric acid, in whole or part, in the superphosphate process. The most promising method of manufacturing cheap oxalic acid on a large scale is by nitric acid oxidation of plant wastes, provided that most of the nitric acid can be recovered for re‐use. With wood and straw waste, oxalic acid yields of up to 85% are obtained at optimum conditions of temperature, catalyst concentration and reaction time. However, the loss of nitric acid is sufficiently low only when fuming nitric acid is used. In this case the ratio of oxalic acid produced to nitric acid lost is 7:1. The nitric acid lost is converted largely into nitrous oxide and this conversion appears to be connected with the oxidation of the lignin fraction of the sawdust.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

