<owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="#Publication_50">
  <rdf:type rdf:resource="#Publication"/>
  <reportsReaction rdf:resource="#reaction_instance_50"/>
  <rdfs:label rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Publication_50</rdfs:label>
  <openalexId rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">W2580250552</openalexId>
  <score rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#decimal">0.98472816</score>
  <publicationDate rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">2014-01-01</publicationDate>
  <rdfs:comment rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Title: A Novel Catalytic System for the Removal of NOx from Lean-Burn Gasoline Engines</rdfs:comment>
  <rdfs:comment rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">NOx gases are among the most harmful gases emitted from mobile and stationary sources that use the hydrocarbons as the fuel in lean-burn conditions. Ammonia and urea have been applied to convert NO to N2 in lean-burn conditions successfully. However, the related problems such as ammonia slipping and facilities corrosion suggest the preferred solution is selective catalytic reduction of NO by hydrocarbon due to its simplicity and availability of hydrocarbons in the exhaust. So far, various forms of catalysts have been evaluated. However, a catalytic system with sufficient activity, selectivity or stability to satisfy the needs has not been developed and is still far away from practical application. In this work, efforts were carried out to develop an efficient catalyst with high surface area, high stability and enough acidity. A series of Zr-SBA-15-Ni catalysts have been prepared by a novel method. The selective catalytic reduction of NO with propane in the presence of excess oxygen was investigated. The reactant mixtures cover various gas compositions (NO: C3H8: O2 = 500ppm: 500–2000ppm: 6-10 vol. %) at the temperature range of 250 to 450 ⁰C. Among the various catalysts prepared, Zr-SBA-Ni-2.5 exhibited the highest activity with 100% conversion of NO to N2 and O2 in the presence of 10 vol. % oxygen, 1750 ppm propane and 500 ppm NO at 450 ⁰C.</rdfs:comment>
</owl:NamedIndividual>

